In C++, you can use a macro to run the same code inline on multiple named variables. Macros are a preprocessor feature that allows you to define code snippets that can be reused throughout your program. Here's an example of how you can create a macro to run the same code on multiple variables:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

#define RUN_CODE(x) x = x * 2; std::cout << x << std::endl;

int main() {
    int a, b, c;
    a = 5;
    b = 10;
    c = 15;

    RUN_CODE(a);
    RUN_CODE(b);
    RUN_CODE(c);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `RUN_CODE` macro takes a variable as an argument and runs the code `x = x * 2; std::cout << x << std::endl;` on it. When you call `RUN_CODE(a)`, `RUN_CODE(b)`, and `RUN_CODE(c)`, the same code is executed for each variable, effectively running the same code inline on multiple named variables.

